00:02:51.680 --> 00:02:54.480
The Paradox of Plenty tries to explain why countries,

00:02:54.480 --> 00:02:57.320
That sit on massive and super rare natural resources,

00:02:57.320 --> 00:03:00.080
Also happen to be the same ones that top the lists of:

00:03:00.140 --> 00:03:03.060
Hey! Our economies are in the pooper! Most of the time.

00:03:03.060 --> 00:03:05.240
Take countries like Saudi Arabia for instance,

00:03:05.240 --> 00:03:07.820
Which is the world's largest exporter of oil.

00:03:07.820 --> 00:03:09.900
Yet, millions of Saudis live in poverty,

00:03:09.900 --> 00:03:12.600
earning less than $530 a month.

00:03:12.740 --> 00:03:15.660
And Saudi Arabia's unemployment is 12.7%,

00:03:15.720 --> 00:03:17.560
Which is HUGE!

00:03:17.640 --> 00:03:21.800
The Paradox of Plenty says that even though one area of the economy is BOOMING,

00:03:21.940 --> 00:03:25.580
Because of a natural resource, other areas lag behind.

00:03:25.640 --> 00:03:29.980
Because there's a disproportionate amount of resources going to that one booming industry.

00:03:30.080 --> 00:03:33.600
Ultimately, this leads to prices of some goods and services in the country.

00:03:33.620 --> 00:03:36.720
Being WAY outta whack compared to other areas.

